      "content": "Here's a timeline focusing on the FLN and the Algerian people's struggle against French colonial rule:\n\n1945: The National Liberation Front (FLN) is founded by a group of Algerian nationalists, including Habib Burgiba and Mohamed Boudiaf, with the goal of gaining independence from France.\n\n1952: The FLN launches the Algiers Case, a wave of protests and strikes that leads to the execution of 21 FLN leaders, further galvanizing Algerian resistance to French colonial rule.\n\n1953: The FLN establishes the Central Committee, with representatives from various Algerian provinces, to coordinate the fight against the French.\n\n1954: The Algerian Revolution officially begins on November 1st with the assassination of a French pied-noir (French colonist) by an FLN member, Markos Belkaeded, and the subsequent explosion of the FLN's Groupe d'Action Armée Alger (GAA).\n\n1955-1961: The FLN wages an armed insurgency against the French colonial authorities, implementing the \"Strategy of the Hundred-Head\" tactic, where hundreds of villagers would be released to later kill scores of French soldiers or functions of the French provincial Government.\n\n1961: The",
